{
  "requirement": "A `{req}` opening fence MAY contain four or more colons. Habeas MUST accept opening fences of any length of at least three colons. A colon-only line with fewer colons than the opener MUST remain inside the requirement body, allowing a nested colon directive. A closing fence outside a fenced code example with at least as many colons as the opener MUST end the body.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment:** `requirements.ci.fence_lengths` is satisfied. The implementation covers every obligation, it is marked as `{impl}`, and the tests cover the interesting cases.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `colon_fence` (src/lib.rs:664–673) is marked `{impl}`. It counts leading colons on a line and accepts any run of three or more, so opening fences of four, seven or more colons are recognised. It returns both the text after the colons and the colon count.\n- `definition_blocks` (src/lib.rs:520–619) is marked `{impl}`. It reads the opener's colon count as `fence_length` and looks for the first later line that meets all three of these conditions:\n  - It is not inside a Markdown code block. Code blocks come from pulldown-cmark offsets, so a `:::` inside a fenced code example is ignored.\n  - It is a colon fence with `count >= fence_length`.\n  - It has nothing after the colons except whitespace.\n- A shorter colon line, such as a nested `:::` closer, is therefore never a closer. It stays in the body, and a nested `:::{note}` opener stays there too. A line with at least as many colons ends the body.\n- CI validation reaches this logic through `collect_definitions`, which calls `definition_blocks` (src/evidence.rs:356). The fence logic itself lives in the two marked functions, so no fence-length behaviour is left unmarked.\n- Other behaviour in these functions is the indent allowance of up to three spaces, the rule that trailing text prevents closing, and name validation. These come from other requirements, such as `requirements.ci.span` and `requirements.ci.identifiers`, which the same code also cites.\n\n**Tests**\n- `a_longer_colon_fence_contains_a_shorter_nested_directive` (src/lib.rs:1258) checks a `::::` opener with a nested `:::{note}` ... `:::`. The body span runs to the `::::` closer, the block is closed, and the text after it stays outside the requirement.\n- `validates_four_or_more_colon_fences_and_longer_closers` (src/evidence.rs:2199) runs end to end through `validate_repository`, using these opener/closer pairs:\n  - `::::` / `::::`\n  - `::::` / `:::::`\n  - `:::::::` / `::::::::`\n  The body contains a nested `:::` directive, and the test checks that the recorded requirement text matches the body with no errors.\n- `colon_requirement_span_includes_markdown_and_excludes_following_text` (src/evidence.rs:2133) puts a `:::` line inside a fenced code example in the body. That line must not close the requirement, and the later `:::` must.\n- Related tests in `closing_fences_allow_no_trailing_text...` and `reports_a_missing_closing_colon_fence` cover the boundaries of closing, but they are cited for `requirements.ci.span` and `requirements.ci.definition_errors`.\n\n**Gaps:** none found, and no FIXMEs are present.",
